{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 /workspace/tools.py read paper:din --start-line 245 --lines 42\npython3 /workspace/tools.py resolve paper:dmr'","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"paper:din\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06978v4\",\n  \"version\": \"content-sha256:5fee25f33f375e51b3ee9417d376ff9dbe2ea3628c932a7679ddfeb0feaaade2\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived exact-version primary text; caching does not establish a local mechanism result\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"83fb9eeca31e2fa3c557b6f08b893ad0018cefb33da6e00952136b9ce17e607e\",\n  \"total_lines\": 831,\n  \"start_line\": 245,\n  \"next_line\": 287,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"245: Loss\\n246: The objective function used in base model is the negative log-likelihood function defined as:\\n247: (2)\\n248: L=−1N∑(𝒙,y)∈𝒮(ylogp(𝒙)+(1−y)log(1−p(𝒙))),L=-\\\\frac{1}{N}\\\\sum_{(\\\\bm{x},y)\\\\in\\\\mathcal{S}}(y\\\\log p(\\\\bm{x})+(1-y)\\\\log(1-p(\\\\bm{x}))),\\n249: where 𝒮\\\\mathcal{S} is the training set of size NN, with 𝒙\\\\bm{x} as the input of the network and y∈{0,1}y\\\\in\\\\{0,1\\\\} as the label, p⁡(𝒙)p(\\\\bm{x}) is the output of the network after the softmax layer, representing the predicted probability of sample 𝒙\\\\bm{x} being clicked.\\n250: 4.3. The structure of Deep Interest Network\\n251: Among all those features of Table 1, user behavior features are critically important and play key roles in modeling user interests in the scenario of e-commerce applications.\\n252: Base model obtains a fixed-length representation vector of user interests by pooling all the embedding vectors over the user behavior feature group, as Eq.(1). This representation vector stays the same for a given user, in regardless of what candidate ads are.\\n253: In this way, the user representation vector with a limited dimension will be a bottleneck to express user’s diverse interests.\\n254: To make it capable enough, an easy method is to expand the dimension of embedding vector, which unfortunately will increase the size of learning parameters heavily.\\n255: It will lead to overfitting under limited training data and add the burden of computation and storage, which may not be tolerated for an industrial online system.\\n256: Is there an elegant way to represent user’s diverse interests in one vector under limited dimension?\\n257: The local activation characteristic of user interests gives us inspiration to design a novel model named deep interest network(DIN).\\n258: Imagine when the young mother mentioned above in section 3 visits the e-commerce site, she finds the displayed new handbag cute and clicks it.\\n259: Let’s dissect the driving force of click action.\\n260: The displayed ad hits the related interests of this young mother by soft-searching her historical behaviors and finding that she had browsed similar goods of tote bag and leather handbag recently.\\n261: In other words, behaviors related to displayed ad greatly contribute to the click action.\\n262: DIN simulates this process by paying attention to the representation of locally activated interests w.r.t. given ad.\\n263: Instead of expressing all user’s diverse interests with the same vector, DIN adaptively calculate the representation vector of user interests by taking into consideration the relevance of historical behaviors w.r.t. candidate ad.\\n264: This representation vector varies over different ads.\\n265: The right part of Fig.2 illustrates the architecture of DIN.\\n266: Compared with base model, DIN introduces a novel designed local activation unit and maintains the other structures the same. Specifically, activation units are applied on the user behavior features, which performs as a weighted sum pooling to adaptively calculate user representation 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} given a candidate ad AA, as shown in Eq.(3)\\n267: (3)\\n268: 𝒗U(A)=f(𝒗A,𝒆1,𝒆2,..,𝒆H)=∑j=1Ha⁡(𝒆j,𝒗A)​𝒆j=∑j=1H𝒘j​𝒆j,\\\\displaystyle\\\\begin{split}\\\\bm{v}_{U}(A)=f(\\\\bm{v}_{A},\\\\bm{e}_{1},\\\\bm{e}_{2},..,\\\\bm{e}_{H})&=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{H}a(\\\\bm{e}_{j},\\\\bm{v}_{A})\\\\bm{e}_{j}=\\\\sum_{j=1}^{H}\\\\bm{w}_{j}\\\\bm{e}_{j},\\\\end{split}\\n269: where {𝒆1,𝒆2,…,𝒆H}\\\\{\\\\bm{e}_{1},\\\\bm{e}_{2},...,\\\\bm{e}_{H}\\\\} is the list of embedding vectors of behaviors of user UU with length of HH, 𝒗A\\\\bm{v}_{A} is the embedding vector of ad AA. In this way, 𝒗U​(A)\\\\bm{v}_{U}(A) varies over different ads. a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot) is a feed-forward network with output as the activation weight, as illustrated in Fig.2. Apart from the two input embedding vectors, a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot) adds the out product of them to feed into the subsequent network, which is an explicit knowledge to help relevance modeling.\\n270: Local activation unit of Eq.(3) shares similar ideas with attention methods which are developed in NMT task(Bahdanau\\n271: et al., 2015).\\n272: However different from traditional attention method, the constraint of ∑iwi=1\\\\sum_{i}w_{i}=1 is relaxed in Eq.(3), aiming to reserve the intensity of user interests.\\n273: That is, normalization with softmax on the output of a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot) is abandoned.\\n274: Instead, value of ∑iwi\\\\sum_{i}w_{i} is treated as an approximation of the intensity of activated user interests to some degree.\\n275: For example, if one user’s historical behaviors contain 90% clothes and 10% electronics.\\n276: Given two candidate ads of T-shirt and phone, T-shirt activates most of the historical behaviors belonging to clothes and may get larger value of 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} (higher intensity of interest) than phone.\\n277: Traditional attention methods lose the resolution on the numerical scale of 𝒗U\\\\bm{v}_{U} by normalizing of the output of a⁡(⋅)a(\\\\cdot).\\n278: We have tried LSTM to model user historical behavior data in the sequential manner.\\n279: But it shows no improvement.\\n280: Different from text which is under the constraint of grammar in NLP task, the sequence of user historical behaviors may contain multiple concurrent interests.\\n281: Rapid jumping and sudden ending over these interests causes the sequence data of user behaviors to seem to be noisy.\\n282: A possible direction is to design special structures to model such data in a sequence way.\\n283: We leave it for future research.\\n284: 5. Training Techniques\\n285: In the advertising system in Alibaba, numbers of goods and users scale up to hundreds of millions.\\n286: Practically, training industrial deep networks with large scale sparse input features is of great challenge.\"\n}\n{\n  \"id\": \"paper:dmr\",\n  \"title\": \"Deep Match to Rank Model for Personalized Click-Through Rate Prediction\",\n  \"url\": \"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/5346\",\n  \"author_code\": \"https://github.com/lvze92/DMR/tree/2c6e76804281954a3c969010a31712cd5054aca9\",\n  \"status\": \"author README field warning verified; publisher full-text fetch blocked, so no full-paper verification is asserted\",\n  \"scientific_status\": \"external_reference\",\n  \"local_reproduction\": false,\n  \"cached_unix\": 1793799696.5657163,\n  \"text_sha256\": \"656c9e5eec79c9843e907a555ede2bf0a66a0db3515916af3d655080057c5a75\",\n  \"content_sha256\": \"656c9e5eec79c9843e907a555ede2bf0a66a0db3515916af3d655080057c5a75\",\n  \"retrieved_url\": \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/lvze92/DMR/2c6e76804281954a3c969010a31712cd5054aca9/README.md\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"author README only, not the blocked publisher paper\",\n  \"version\": \"content-sha256:656c9e5eec79c9843e907a555ede2bf0a66a0db3515916af3d655080057c5a75\",\n  \"text_ref\": \"source-text://paper:dmr\",\n  \"text_lines\": 9\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport hashlib,ast,importlib.util\nfor f in ['candidate.py','baseline_reference.py','history_g1_r0019_pooled_observed.py','history_g3_r0023_tied_target.py','history_g3_r0023_tied_context.py']:\n s=open(f,'rb').read();print(f,hashlib.sha256(s).hexdigest())\nprint('torch available',importlib.util.find_spec('torch') is not None)\nt=ast.parse(open('candidate.py').read())\nfor n in t.body:\n if isinstance(n,ast.ClassDef):\n  if n.name=='_ParentModel':\n   for m in n.body:\n    if isinstance(m,ast.FunctionDef) and m.name in ['_shared_features','_membership']:\n     print(ast.get_source_segment(open('candidate.py').read(),m))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport hashlib,ast,importlib.util\nfor f in ['candidate.py','baseline_reference.py','history_g1_r0019_pooled_observed.py','history_g3_r0023_tied_target.py','history_g3_r0023_tied_context.py']:\n s=open(f,'rb').read();print(f,hashlib.sha256(s).hexdigest())\nprint('torch available',importlib.util.find_spec('torch') is not None)\nt=ast.parse(open('candidate.py').read())\nfor n in t.body:\n if isinstance(n,ast.ClassDef):\n  if n.name=='_ParentModel':\n   for m in n.body:\n    if isinstance(m,ast.FunctionDef) and m.name in ['_shared_features','_membership']:\n     print(ast.get_source_segment(open('candidate.py').read(),m))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\ncandidate.py c7afb27541d642ee27bce4d54755406b29a3605d140453a71e8c9087882a123b\nbaseline_reference.py ee10868f589c077b19f57a1fb07702c947c56e970994cdc537ef83ea2b8bc943\nhistory_g1_r0019_pooled_observed.py ca68bdb11d082057b8c7984e212e745331b2f33d4e8895dc4488efe13ad3ed02\nhistory_g3_r0023_tied_target.py 6d9e6c4427d4aebede81f91520c6dbb6449d95cd48cde749f5b9af59662e0fa9\nhistory_g3_r0023_tied_context.py 10c1cfc2dc0ff88521ddae66ab3089e2289fdd7bc30079eaf1969b841806193c\ntorch available False\ndef _membership(self, batch):\n        mask = batch['history_mask']\n        target_c = batch['static'][:, self.category:self.category + 1]\n        target_b = batch['static'][:, self.brand:self.brand + 1]\n        hc, hb = batch['history_category'], batch['history_brand']\n        cm = mask & (target_c > 0) & (hc > 0) & (hc == target_c)\n        bm = mask & (target_b > 0) & (hb > 0) & (hb == target_b)\n        semantic = torch.stack([\n            mask & ~cm & ~bm, cm & ~bm, bm & ~cm, cm & bm\n        ], dim=1).float()  # [B,4,L]\n        sizes = semantic.sum(-1)\n        if self.grouping_mode == 'rank':\n            # Assign in supplied input order, counting only valid positions.\n            # Prefix thresholds reproduce true cardinalities even with gaps.\n            # This is a positional replacement, not a randomized control.\n            rank = mask.long().cumsum(-1) - 1\n            upper = sizes.long().cumsum(-1)\n            lower = upper - sizes.long()\n            membership = ((rank[:, None, :] >= lower[:, :, None]) &\n                          (rank[:, None, :] < upper[:, :, None]) &\n                          mask[:, None, :]).float()\n        else:\n            membership = semantic\n        return membership, semantic, sizes\ndef _shared_features(self, batch, scope=None, layout=None, direction=None):\n        scope = self.field_scope if scope is None else scope\n        layout = self.field_layout if layout is None else layout\n        direction = self.global_direction if direction is None else direction\n        fields = [embedding(batch['static'][:, i])\n                  for i, embedding in enumerate(self.embeddings)]\n        static = torch.cat(fields, dim=-1)\n        _, semantic, sizes = self._membership(batch)\n        mask = batch['history_mask']\n        dim = self.config['embedding_dim']\n        if mask.shape[1] == 0:\n            pools = static.new_zeros((static.shape[0], 4, dim))\n            moments = torch.zeros_like(pools)\n            means = static.new_zeros((static.shape[0], 4 if scope == 'rank' else 1, 4, dim))\n        else:\n            ages = (torch.log2(batch['history_age_seconds'] + 1).long() + 1).clamp(0, 32)\n            # Each history field is embedded exactly once in forward. Static\n            # target embeddings are separate lookup consumers, as in ancestor.\n            cv = self.embeddings[self.category](batch['history_category'])\n            bv = self.embeddings[self.brand](batch['history_brand'])\n            av = self.action(batch['history_type'])\n            rv = self.recency(ages)\n            history = cv + bv + av + rv\n            denominator = sizes.clamp_min(1).unsqueeze(-1)\n            pools = torch.bmm(semantic, history) / denominator\n            # Keep the parent's operation ordering for both marginal means.\n            content_mean = torch.bmm(semantic, cv + bv) / denominator\n            context_mean = torch.bmm(semantic, av + rv) / denominator\n            moments = 0.5 * (content_mean * context_mean)\n            values = torch.cat([cv, bv, av, rv], dim=-1)\n            if scope == 'rank':\n                rank = mask.long().cumsum(-1) - 1\n                upper = sizes.long().cumsum(-1)\n                lower = upper - sizes.long()\n                membership = ((rank[:, None, :] >= lower[:, :, None]) &\n                              (rank[:, None, :] < upper[:, :, None]) &\n                              mask[:, None, :]).float()\n                field_means = torch.bmm(membership, values) / denominator\n                means = field_means.reshape(static.shape[0], 4, 4, dim)\n            else:\n                membership = mask[:, None, :].float()\n                denominator = mask.float().sum(-1).clamp_min(1)[:, None, None]\n                field_means = torch.bmm(membership, values) / denominator\n                means = field_means.reshape(static.shape[0], 1, 4, dim)\n        counts = self._match_counts(batch)\n        statistics = self._statistics(counts)\n        histogram = self._histogram(batch, semantic, sizes)[0]\n        full, collapsed = self._contrast_blocks(means)\n        selected = full if direction == 'contrast' else collapsed\n        if scope == 'global' and layout == 'wide':\n            # Isometry R^48 -> R^192: four copies scaled by 1/sqrt(4).\n            # All columns are used, but effective input rank remains <=48.\n            extra = selected.expand(-1, 4, -1, -1) * 0.5\n        else:\n            extra = selected\n        features = torch.cat([static, pools.flatten(1), statistics, histogram,\n                              moments.flatten(1), extra.flatten(1)], dim=-1)\n        return features, extra, means, full, collapsed, semantic, sizes\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
